//! check: run(exit=1, stdout="handled: refused\nerror: invalid")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.fs, err.row, err.propagate, err.else
//!
//! The `invalid` litmus — the second tag s90 added here — witnessed the
//! same way `not_found` and `exists` are: an `else` that handles it, and
//! a `?` that rides it out of `main` so the RECORD names the tag rather
//! than this repository asserting its own reading of it.
//!
//! What `invalid` means and why it is a row at all: the byte currency is
//! `List[int]` (§11's recorded interim), so a caller can spell an element
//! that is not a byte, and the builtin tier answers `invalid` rather than
//! writing something the caller did not mean. §11's encoder rule has
//! `hex.encode` TRAP on the same input, and the difference is decided by
//! §14, which is the stronger rule for an os module: the row vocabulary
//! is the toolchain's, adopted verbatim, and a library that traps where
//! the toolchain raises has taken a decision away from its caller.
//!
//! Both directions of the range are here — a negative element and one
//! above 255 — because "is not a byte" has two edges and a check written
//! for one of them is a check that passes the other.
//!
//! `copy_file` cannot raise this tag and does not declare it: its bytes
//! came out of `read_bytes` and are bytes by construction. That absence
//! is a signature, not an omission, and `std.fs`'s doc says so on both
//! functions.

use std.fs

fn main() -> !int {
    var high = List[int]()
    (mut high).push(119)
    (mut high).push(256)
    var handled = "not-handled"
    fs.write_bytes("invalid-row.bin", high) else |_| {
        handled = "refused"
    }
    print("handled: {handled}")
    var low = List[int]()
    (mut low).push(0 - 1)
    fs.write_bytes("invalid-row.bin", low)?
    print("unreachable")
    0
}
